          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:8e65b1b2f81546fcc4622224b80873cec5d113344a1f41df5533ad5442d8e734:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_b555e7c24bd3424fb018d5ff28ca8ef2",
      "statement": "The acknowledgement rule (RPO=0): a gate holds each write response until a durability proof covers it; after a bucket proof, celld re-reads the ownership record and acknowledges only if it still names this node at this epoch (a record read, not a clock comparison, so pauses or skewed clocks cannot pass it); a fleet proof skips the read because followers fsync and a takeover seals the prior node-log session first.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/guarantees.md#L133-L149",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:fb54a91ed163a0bd56afef262e350d459528f328e571fecbed201ff86a67337b:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_8208a75dd1564540b7df06f14625065d",
      "statement": "A node recruits up to two followers (never itself); one follower suffices, so a fleet needs two running nodes before any fleet proof completes; three or more nodes hold three copies; and the ensemble keeps acknowledging while one follower remains — no per-loss bucket fallback.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/guarantees.md#L151-L167",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:eb245ff4ad1ea390df353aa38d2ebb6a52afc2733b5578dff742499c2e66d139:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_13120950e760467e90b781d1f56e98f1",
      "statement": "Because fleet proofs can acknowledge before the bucket upload finishes, each process session creates a conditional node-log record before its first fleet-durable acknowledgement; a cold activation must then resolve absent/sealed/open records (open or recovering triggers a recovery sequence of CAS-fencing, sealing followers, uploading retained segments, and marking sealed) before it can restore.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/guarantees.md#L168-L183",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:67e4434527bbb7d09b00cc60a8f27982deee7fbe9608b77972faf71fc9ae92fb:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_ffbf377d3b6244afa970eb4ca968b061",
      "statement": "A restore selects the newest epoch prefix containing LTX data and reads the full contiguous chain from transaction zero (epoch-seal objects gone; legacy e<epoch>.seal.json does not limit the chain), because a fenced node can append an unacknowledged tail to an older prefix and a full-chain read is what prevents hiding a later-acknowledged tail.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/guarantees.md#L185-L198",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:706453cc7954b75b23743bc2151b8252348b1152253ed70663ccbef623504817:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_23f94c66482540bcb5ae1dfc58df49d0",
      "statement": "The fencing argument is verified rather than asserted: TLA+ model checking covers one-writer-per-epoch and no-lost-acknowledged-write, including the epoch-prefix lineage argument, and live-fleet fault tests confirm a frozen owner whose lease moved refuses to serve old state and a bucket-cut-off node fences itself.",
